\section{Reading your own log as numbers}
\label{tut:metrics}

This tutorial turns the recorded project back on its author.
The previous tutorials read the log as a sequence---play it back, jump
to a tag---and this one reads it as summaries: how many days and runs,
how often an error was followed by the same error, how large and how
branched the program is, and whether any of that is moving.
The invariant is the same recorded project; what varies across the
steps is the \emph{question} asked of it, one metric family at a time,
so the learner can attribute each new number to the question that
produced it rather than to a change in the data.

Two design choices shape every step.
First, the learner makes the history before measuring it: the opening
step has them run a program that fails twice in the same way and then
succeeds, so that every later number has a visible cause the learner
remembers typing.
Second, each step asks for a prediction before the command---how many
sessions, which error series will be higher, whether the trend is
decidable yet---because a number the learner has guessed at first is
one they read, and a number that merely appears is one they skim.
The order runs from the metric with the most obvious meaning (days and
runs) to the one that needs the most caveats (trends), and ends where
the playback tutorial ended: in [[learnlog list]], seeing that the
reflection itself was recorded.

Every metric step closes with a \emph{How to read it} part, because a
number the learner cannot interpret is worse than none: an error
quotient shown without a reading invites the one reading the literature
rules out, a grade.
The readings are kept to what the research supports.
A low quotient means errors were rare or fixed at once, which is what
fluency looks like---but the quotient was tuned on students meeting
the same error repeatedly~\autocite{jadud2006methods}, so a low value
on easy problems says nothing, and the reading says so.
The two series are read as a progression, since syntax errors carried
signal early in a course and runtime errors
later~\autocite{svabensky2024comparison}.
Time is read through its spread rather than its sum, because within the
same students the better work was started earlier, not worked on
longer~\autocite{edwards2009comparing}, and coarse time-on-task did not
correlate with results~\autocite{leinonen2022timeontask}.
The pair of quotient and time is read the way our own earlier work
read it~\autocite{snider2023edit}: high effort with a falling quotient
is practice paying off, high effort with a high quotient is
perseverance through a hard problem, and low effort with a low
quotient is the ambiguous corner where both the fluent and the
disengaged sit.
Complexity is read against the finding that most novice programs have
none~\autocite{Keuning2017}, so its first appearance is growth; lint
counts are read as a to-do list to be worked one rule at a time,
because students nudged on everything at once added more findings than
they removed~\autocite{Bobadilla2024} while a single targeted rule
fell and stayed low~\autocite{Ostlund2023}.
None of the readings compares the learner with anyone but their
earlier self~\autocite{jivet2018license}, and none is offered as a
prediction~\autocite{tabanao2011predicting}.
The novice variant gives the readings as plain advice; the expert
variant names the studies they rest on.

\subsection{Two variants, one contract}

As in \cref{tut:getting-started}, the tutorial tangles into a novice
entry, [[metrics]], and an expert entry, [[metrics-expert]], sharing
every step's checks through common chunks.
The mechanism the expert variant makes visible is the definition
behind each number: what a session is and where the twenty minutes
come from, which pairs of runs the error quotient scores, which tool
counts the complexity and why the maintainability index carries a
warning, how a linter is found, and what rule turns a series into the
word \enquote{rising}.
The novice variant keeps to reading the tables and never names a
paper or a tool.

