\subsection{Produce a few runs to measure}

The opening step manufactures the data the rest of the tutorial reads,
and does so deliberately: a run that fails, the same failure after an
edit, then success.
Its object of learning is that the numbers to come describe
\emph{these} runs---the learner will recognise the repeated
[[NameError]] again in the error quotient step.
The check is only that the program was run; how many times and with
which mistakes is the learner's to arrange, since the point is that
they know what they did.
The expert extra names what the log keeps per run, because the
definitions in later steps are stated in those terms.

<<shared step: produce a few runs to measure>>=
# Produce a few runs to measure

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'python\S*\s+\S+\.py'
hint: Run your program with `python` a few times, fixing it between runs.
```

In your learnlog project, write a short program that uses a variable
before defining it, for example:

```python
print(total)
```

Run it with `python` and watch it fail with a `NameError`.
Change something small---add a comment, say---and run it again, so that
it fails the same way twice.
Then define the variable above the `print` and run it a third time.
<<novice step: produce a few runs to measure>>=
<<shared step: produce a few runs to measure>>
<<expert step: produce a few runs to measure>>=
<<shared step: produce a few runs to measure>>

Each run leaves two commits in the hidden repository: a header with
the code as it was about to run, and a trailer with the exit code, the
traceback if there was one, and the end time.
Every number in this tutorial is computed from those fields alone; the
metrics never read the exit code to decide whether a run failed, only
the traceback, because `sys.exit(1)` is not a mistake and a caught
exception exits with zero.
@

\subsection{Summarise your time and effort}

The step's object of learning is the shape of a metrics report: a
header stating the window and the settings, then one row per period.
The learner is asked to predict the session count before looking,
which is what makes the idle threshold in the header a thing they
notice rather than a line they skip.
The expert extra gives the definition and its source: a session ends
at twenty minutes without a run, measured from the end of a run, and
the clock counts run-to-run activity only.

<<shared step: summarise your time and effort>>=
# Summarise your time and effort

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+metrics\s+time\b'
hint: Run `learnlog metrics time --period all`.
```

Before you run anything: how many sessions do you think your runs so
far make---one, or several?
Then run `learnlog metrics time --period all`.
The first lines say which window the report covers and what counts as
a break between sessions; the row below counts your days, runs and
sessions, how many runs failed, how many lines you added and removed,
and how long you were active from run to run.
Compare the session count with your guess.

## How to read it

More is not better on its own.
Look at `days` and `first run` first: they show how you spread your
work, and spreading work over more days and starting earlier is what
distinguishes students' better work from their weaker work---not the
number of hours.
`runs` and `+lines`/`-lines` show how much you actually did in the
window.
`active time` is only the clock around your runs, so read it as a
rough lower bound, and never as the time you spent thinking.
<<novice step: summarise your time and effort>>=
<<shared step: summarise your time and effort>>

By default `learnlog metrics time` covers the last seven days; `--period
today`, `--period month` and `--period all` change that.
<<expert step: summarise your time and effort>>=
<<shared step: summarise your time and effort>>

A session ends when twenty minutes pass between the end of one run and
the start of the next; that is the convention used for program-run
data in the ProgSnap2 literature, and it is a parameter (`--idle`)
because estimates like this one change materially with the threshold.
Active time adds up each run's duration (capped at the threshold, so a
program waiting on `input()` for an hour does not count an hour) and
the gaps between runs in a session, and credits nothing before the
first run.
It is labelled run-to-run for that reason: it is not the time you
spent thinking.
By default the report covers the last seven days; `--period all`, an
explicit `--since`/`--until`, or `--by day|week|month` for a series
change that.

The reading rests on three findings.
Within the same students, higher-scored work was started earlier and
finished earlier, with no more time spent (Edwards et al., 2009);
coarse time-on-task---first event to last---did not correlate with
weekly results at all (Leinonen et al., 2022); and run and edit counts
track active time closely (Leinonen et al., 2017), which is why the
counts come before the clock.
@

\subsection{Score the repeated errors}

This is the step the opening step was staged for.
Its object of learning is that the error quotient measures
\emph{repetition}, not failure: the pair of identical [[NameError]]
runs scores the maximum, the pair ending in success scores nothing.
The prediction contrasts the two series the report prints---syntax
errors only, and all errors---and the learner's history contains no
syntax error, so the contrast is decided by their own data.
The expert extra states the scoring and its parameters, and the
caveat every report prints.

<<shared step: score the repeated errors>>=
# Score the repeated errors

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+metrics\s+eq\b'
hint: Run `learnlog metrics eq --period all`.
```

The error quotient (EQ) looks at consecutive runs of the same program
and asks: did both end in an error, and was it the same kind of error?
Before running it, predict which of the two columns will be higher for
your history: `EQ (syntax)`, which counts only syntax errors, or
`EQ (all errors)`, which counts every uncaught exception.
Then run `learnlog metrics eq --period all` and check.
`pairs` tells you how many consecutive runs were compared, and `RED`
counts how long your streaks of the same error were.

## How to read it

EQ runs from 0 to 1.
Higher means your runs often ended in an error right after another
error, and often the same kind; lower means errors were rare or you
fixed each one in one go.
A low EQ on a problem that was hard for you is a good sign: you seem
to know what you are doing.
A low EQ can also mean the problems were too easy to make you stumble,
so always read it next to what you were working on---if it stays low
week after week, try something harder.
A high EQ is what a hard bug looks like from the outside; it is not a
grade.
If RED is high as well, one error kept coming back: stop, read the
error message and the line it points to, and change one thing between
runs.
The two columns tell you where you are: syntax errors dominate while
the language is new; later the runtime errors (`NameError`,
`TypeError`, wrong values) are the ones with something to say, so an
`EQ (syntax)` that falls while `EQ (all errors)` stays up is normal
progress, not a setback.
<<novice step: score the repeated errors>>=
<<shared step: score the repeated errors>>

These numbers describe how your runs followed one another.
They are not a grade, and a high value after a hard bug is exactly
what a hard bug looks like.
<<expert step: score the repeated errors>>=
<<shared step: score the repeated errors>>

Two consecutive runs of the same script form a pair only if the code
changed between them---the header commits have different trees---so
re-running unchanged code is not a repeated mistake.
A pair scores 13 if both runs ended in an error and 3 more if the
error type is the same, out of a possible 16, and EQ is the mean over
pairs; that is the two-parameter form used in the Python literature,
and the header names it so that two reports can be compared only when
they were computed the same way.
RED is Becker's repeated error density: for each streak of `r`
consecutive repeats it adds `r^2/(r+1)`, so one long streak weighs more
than two short ones.
Neither number is a score of you; the literature that introduced them
could not predict who was at risk with them, and the header says so.

The readings come from the studies behind the metric.
Jadud (2006) built EQ after seeing that struggling students met the
same error again and again, which is why a high value reads as a
hard patch rather than carelessness.
Švábenský et al. (2024) found that syntax errors predicted early
exams and runtime errors later ones, which is why the two series are
read as a progression.
Our own earlier work (Snider et al., 2023) read EQ together with
time-on-task: high effort with a low or falling EQ is practice paying
off, high effort with a high EQ is perseverance through a hard
problem, low effort with a high EQ is the pattern of the students who
struggled most, and low effort with a low EQ is ambiguous---the fluent
and the disengaged both sit there, which is why a persistently low EQ
is an invitation to pick harder problems.
Tabanao, Rodrigo and Jadud (2011) could not identify at-risk students
from these numbers, and Karvelas et al. (2020) showed the values shift
with the tools used, so the only comparison that means anything is
with your own earlier weeks.
@

\subsection{Measure the code}

The step changes the object of measurement from the history to the
program, which is the contrast that separates the code family from the
two before it: [[code]] reads files, not runs.
Its object of learning is that size and branching are reported
together---lines beside complexity---and that a first program scores
a suspiciously perfect maintainability index, which the header
explains.
The prediction is the maximum complexity, a number the learner can
work out by counting the `if`s and loops in their own file.

<<shared step: measure the code>>=
# Measure the code

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+metrics\s+code\b'
hint: Run `learnlog metrics code`.
```

Count the `if`, `for` and `while` statements in the most complicated
function of your program and add one; that is roughly its cyclomatic
complexity.
Run `learnlog metrics code` and find `max CC` in the row.
The row also gives the number of source lines, of functions, and of
functions with a complexity above one, plus two maintainability
figures; read the header line about the maintainability index before
trusting those.

## How to read it

Most beginners' programs have complexity 1 in every function: they run
straight through.
The first function with `max CC` above one is your program starting
to make decisions---that is growth, not a problem.
A function whose complexity keeps climbing, past ten or so, is one to
split into smaller functions with names that say what they do.
`SLOC` growing together with complexity is normal; complexity growing
while the lines stay put means your conditions are getting denser,
which is worth a second look.
Treat the maintainability index as a curiosity: it reads 100 for a
tiny program and drops as you write more, without your code getting
worse.
<<novice step: measure the code>>=
<<shared step: measure the code>>

For one window the report measures your files as they are now; with
`--by week` it measures the code as it stood at the end of each week.
<<expert step: measure the code>>=
<<shared step: measure the code>>

The counts come from `radon`: complexity is McCabe's predicates plus
one, counting every `if`, loop, `except`, comprehension clause and
boolean operand beyond the first.
The maintainability index is Coleman's 1994 formula over Halstead
volume, complexity and lines, rescaled to 0--100; it reads 100 for any
program whose Halstead volume is zero---which, with radon's operator
definitions, is every program that only calls functions---and its own
authors warned that comments inflate it in small files.
It is shown because it is widely used, with that caveat in the header.
With `--by week` each row measures the tree of the week's last run,
extracted from the hidden repository, so a file that did not parse
that week shows dashes rather than numbers.

The reading rests on what is known about these numbers for small
programs.
In a corpus of four million novice scripts, 78% had complexity one
and only 4% went above four (Aivaloglou and Hermans, as reported by
Keuning et al., 2017), so the first decision in your code is the
event worth noticing.
Whether complexity says more than size is contested---Jay et al.
(2009) found it nearly redundant with lines of code, Landman et al.
(2016) found it not so at function level---which is why the two are
shown side by side.
The maintainability index's own authors warned that comments inflate
it in small modules (Coleman et al., 1994), its constants have no
derivation (Heitlager et al., 2007), and against measured maintenance
effort only size carried signal (Sjøberg et al., 2012).
@

\subsection{Ask a linter}

A short step whose object of learning is that a metric can be
unavailable without being a failure: the report either counts
findings by rule or says how to install a linter.
The learner does not need a linter to complete the step, which is why
the check accepts either outcome.
The expert extra explains the small rule set and how the tool is
found.

<<shared step: ask a linter>>=
# Ask a linter

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+metrics\s+lint\b'
hint: Run `learnlog metrics lint`.
```

Run `learnlog metrics lint`.
The row counts what the linter found, by rule, and the header names
the linter that ran: `ruff` unless you asked for `pylint`.
Should the header instead say that no linter was found, that answer
completes the step too.

## How to read it

The count by rule is a to-do list, not a score.
Pick the one rule with the most findings and clear those first; then
run `learnlog metrics lint` again.
Trying to fix everything at once tends to add findings faster than it
removes them, while one kind of issue worked on deliberately goes away
and stays away.
An undefined name (`F821`) is a bug waiting to happen; an unused
import or variable is something you meant to use or meant to delete.
<<novice step: ask a linter>>=
<<shared step: ask a linter>>
<<expert step: ask a linter>>=
<<shared step: ask a linter>>

learnlog ships both `ruff` and `pylint` and runs `ruff` by default,
with a deliberately small rule set: undefined names, unused imports
and variables, bare `except`, comparison with `None` by `==`, long
lines.
General-purpose rule sets flood novices with findings that do not
matter to them; a short list is what the code-quality literature
recommends.
`pylint` asks the same questions but infers types on the way, so it
sees a little more and takes a little longer: `--linter pylint` runs
it once, and `learnlog config linter pylint` makes it the project's
default.
A linter you installed yourself in the project's `.venv` is preferred
over the bundled copy.

The one-rule-at-a-time reading comes from two classroom studies:
students nudged about all their quality issues at once added more
violations than they removed (Bobadilla et al., 2024), while quality
improved sharply and durably for the single violation a course made
its explicit focus (Östlund et al., 2023).
@

\subsection{Look for trends}

The final metric step's object of learning is restraint: a trend
needs enough periods to exist, and with a day or two of history the
honest answer is that it is too early to say.
Asking the learner to predict whether [[trends]] will report a
direction makes the minimum-bucket rule the thing they discover.
The expert extra states the rule and the closed vocabulary, and why
the words are the only judgement the report makes.

<<shared step: look for trends>>=
# Look for trends

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+metrics\s+trends\b'
hint: Run `learnlog metrics trends --by day`.
```

`learnlog metrics trends` puts the headline number of every family
side by side, one row per week by default, and follows the table with
one line per metric saying whether it is rising, falling or steady.
You have a day or two of history: do you expect it to say anything
yet?
Run `learnlog metrics trends --by day` to see one row per day and
read the direction lines.

## How to read it

Read the directions in pairs, once there are enough weeks.
EQ falling while lines and complexity rise is the pattern you want:
harder programs with fewer repeated errors.
EQ falling while runs, lines and days also fall may just mean you did
less, or easier, work.
EQ rising together with `max complexity` is what learning a new
construct looks like from the outside; expect it to fall again in a
few weeks, and if it does not, that is the problem to bring to a
teacher.
Rising days with everything else steady is steady practice.
No direction compares you with anyone else, and none of them is a
grade.
<<novice step: look for trends>>=
<<shared step: look for trends>>

Come back to this command later in the course; with a few weeks of
rows the directions appear, and each compares you with your own
earlier weeks and nobody else.
<<expert step: look for trends>>=
<<shared step: look for trends>>

A direction needs at least four periods with a value.
Given those, the report fits a least-squares line over the periods,
scales its slope to the whole span, divides by the median value, and
calls the result rising or falling beyond ten percent and steady
within; the header prints the rule so the word can be checked.
The vocabulary is closed on purpose: whether a falling error quotient
is good news depends on what you were doing, so the report shows the
first and last values beside the word and leaves the judgement to you.

The paired readings follow the trajectory our earlier work described
(Snider et al., 2023): students tend to move from low effort and few
errors, through a stretch of many repeated errors as the problems get
harder, to sustained effort with falling errors, and finally to
solving problems quickly with few errors again---so the same falling
EQ means different things at different points, and only the other
columns say which.
Self-comparison is the frame learners receive best (Jivet et al.,
2018), and no study has managed to predict outcomes from these
numbers (Tabanao et al., 2011), which is why the words stop at
rising, falling and steady.
@

\subsection{Group by milestone}

The step changes the bucket, not the metric: the same summary the
learner has read per day now comes one row per tag, so what varies
is only the slicing, and the learner's own milestone vocabulary
becomes a report axis (\cref{sec:metrics-tags}).
The prediction asks where the first runs land, because the
[[(untagged)]] row is the one part of the table the learner has not
named themselves.

<<shared step: group by milestone>>=
# Group by milestone

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+metrics\s+\w+[^\n]*--by\s+tag'
hint: Run `learnlog metrics time --by tag`.
```

A tag you created (`learnlog tag lab1`) is also a bucket: each tag
starts a period that runs until the next tag.
Before you run it: which row do you expect your very first runs to
land in?
Run `learnlog metrics time --by tag`.

## How to read it

The `(untagged)` row is everything before your first tag; with no
tags at all it is the only row.
Each named row starts at its tag's run and ends just before the next
tag, so the last row is your current, still-open milestone.
<<novice step: group by milestone>>=
<<shared step: group by milestone>>
<<expert step: group by milestone>>=
<<shared step: group by milestone>>

Unlike `--by week`, which assigns a whole work session to the week
of its first run, `--by tag` cuts a session at the tag: the tag is a
boundary you declared, so work on either side belongs to different
milestones---and the error quotient never pairs runs across one.
Tag periods are also unequal in length, which is why
`learnlog metrics trends --by tag` compares milestones rather than
equal stretches of time, and says so in its header.
@

\subsection{See the reflection in the log}

The closing step returns to \cref{tut:playback-and-tagging}'s lesson
with a new member: metrics runs record themselves, as playback does.
Its object of learning is that reading the summaries is part of the
history the summaries describe, and---a contrast the expert variant
makes explicit---that the metrics nevertheless leave their own runs
out of the counts.

<<shared step: see the reflection in the log>>=
# See the reflection in the log

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+list\b'
hint: Run `learnlog list`.
```

Run `learnlog list`.
The `learnlog metrics` commands you just ran appear as runs of their
own, next to your program's runs.
<<novice step: see the reflection in the log>>=
<<shared step: see the reflection in the log>>

Looking back at your work is part of your work, so learnlog records
it; the metrics themselves do not count these runs, only your
program's.
<<expert step: see the reflection in the log>>=
<<shared step: see the reflection in the log>>

`play`, `list` and `metrics` are the review subcommands: they record
a header and a trailer like any run, but capture no output (the output
*is* the log) and, for `metrics`, no view trace (it showed no
commit).
When the metrics read the log they drop learnlog's own runs first,
including the header commit of the very invocation that is running,
so a report never counts itself.
@
